1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,500
₹1,53,382 profit, on a year our rules never saw.

2
00:00:03,533 --> 00:00:05,567
So why aren't we trading it?

3
00:00:07,533 --> 00:00:11,033
Per gram of pure gold, they should cost exactly the same.

4
00:00:11,067 --> 00:00:15,567
Parity measures every gap, every trading day, after every cost.

5
00:00:15,600 --> 00:00:18,367
Your guess: is any gap worth trading today?

6
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:21,567
No. The biggest gap is 40.5 bp. A trade needs 47.6.

7
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:24,000
Most days, the honest answer is wait.

8
00:00:24,267 --> 00:00:26,000
Rewind to January 2026…

9
00:00:26,033 --> 00:00:29,367
…the gold crash. Every gap blew through the cost line.

10
00:00:29,633 --> 00:00:32,500
23 years of official MCX data. 711 contracts.

11
00:00:32,767 --> 00:00:36,100
Every fee. Every tax. And slippage you control.

12
00:00:36,133 --> 00:00:40,133
Past 12 bp of slippage, no result survives.

13
00:00:41,167 --> 00:00:43,667
Most teams would report this…

14
00:00:43,700 --> 00:00:47,033
…we report this. Up to 89% of the headline disappears.

15
00:00:47,300 --> 00:00:51,733
Every trade placed inside its contract's calendar. None held into delivery.

16
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:55,833
Rules frozen in Git. Eight years kept sealed, then opened once.

17
00:00:58,267 --> 00:01:00,500
January 2026 alone made ₹2,39,815.

18
00:01:00,533 --> 00:01:02,800
The other 111 trades lost ₹86,433.

19
00:01:02,833 --> 00:01:05,433
Outside the crash, the rule loses money.

20
00:01:05,700 --> 00:01:08,633
Best of 72 settings? A 65% chance that's noise.

21
00:01:08,667 --> 00:01:11,033
A small edge. Not proven. We say so.

22
00:01:11,333 --> 00:01:14,700
Checked by 10,344 automated tests, before every update.

23
00:01:14,967 --> 00:01:17,600
And it updates itself, every trading day.

24
00:01:17,867 --> 00:01:19,600
That was 6 October.
